# Clean Code — Code Smells

Code smells are symptoms in the source code that indicate a likely design problem beneath the surface. They do not always demand immediate action, but they always warrant attention.

## SMELL-01 — Rigidity

The software is difficult to change. A single modification causes a cascade of subsequent changes in dependent modules. Rigidity often signals that responsibilities are tangled and abstractions are missing.

## SMELL-02 — Fragility

The software breaks in many unrelated places when a single change is made. Fragility indicates that the code makes assumptions about global or shared state that are not enforced through the type system or clear contracts.

## SMELL-03 — Immobility

You cannot reuse a component in another context without dragging along its entangled dependencies. Immobility is the cost of skipping proper abstraction and dependency management.

## SMELL-04 — Needless Complexity

The design contains infrastructure, abstractions, or flexibility that serves no current requirement. Over-engineering for imagined future use cases adds burden without benefit.

## SMELL-05 — Needless Repetition

The same logic appears in multiple places. Repetition means a bug must be fixed in every copy and a change must be made everywhere — violations of the Don't Repeat Yourself principle.

## SMELL-06 — Opacity

The code is hard to understand. The intent behind expressions, names, or structures is not self-evident to a reader who did not write it. Opacity accumulates through poor naming, missing abstraction, and unexplained side effects.
